WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.178 --> 00:00:02.722
[oil rippling]

00:00:05.016 --> 00:00:06.309
Fire!

00:00:06.309 --> 00:00:07.476
[oil exploding]

00:00:07.476 --> 00:00:09.270
Roel Konijnendijk: So,
boiling oil, it didn't happen.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:12.398
Sorry. I mean, you can
just throw boiling water.

00:00:12.398 --> 00:00:13.941
Hi, my name is Roel Konijnendijk.

00:00:13.941 --> 00:00:15.443
I'm a lecturer in ancient history

00:00:15.443 --> 00:00:17.028
at the University of Oxford.

00:00:17.028 --> 00:00:19.113
I'm an expert in ancient
warfare, and I'm here to talk

00:00:19.113 --> 00:00:22.575
to you today about premodern
warfare in modern movies.

00:00:26.329 --> 00:00:29.332
[soldiers charging]

00:00:32.376 --> 00:00:37.215
So, this is the supposed
Greek concept of othismos,

00:00:37.215 --> 00:00:38.925
which literally means pushing.

00:00:38.925 --> 00:00:40.968
And about 100 years
ago, a scholar in Oxford

00:00:40.968 --> 00:00:42.845
who clearly had rugby on his mind

00:00:42.845 --> 00:00:44.555
decided that this should be conceived

00:00:44.555 --> 00:00:46.515
as a literal mass shoving.

00:00:46.515 --> 00:00:48.142
We have no evidence of that.

00:00:48.142 --> 00:00:50.102
Nobody ever says that that's the case,

00:00:50.102 --> 00:00:51.979
but for some reason,
this really caught on.

00:00:51.979 --> 00:00:54.232
And so for the last 100 years,
it's been controversial.

00:00:54.232 --> 00:00:55.191
But generally speaking, I mean,

00:00:55.191 --> 00:00:57.026
people didn't want to fight like that.

00:00:57.026 --> 00:00:58.986
Spears are right in your face.

00:00:58.986 --> 00:01:02.740
So there's a lot of reasons
to want to avoid this.

00:01:03.991 --> 00:01:05.201
[spears clanging]

00:01:06.369 --> 00:01:08.454
So, this moment, where they switch from

00:01:08.454 --> 00:01:09.538
fighting in formation

00:01:09.538 --> 00:01:12.166
to going after individuals
who are still resisting,

00:01:12.166 --> 00:01:14.502
that seemed very realistic
for most of these fights.

00:01:14.502 --> 00:01:16.879
Make sure you can kill as
many of them as possible,

00:01:16.879 --> 00:01:18.256
because that's when they're vulnerable.

00:01:18.256 --> 00:01:19.548
That's when they're not fighting back.

00:01:19.548 --> 00:01:21.842
So that's when you can just
spear them in the back.

00:01:21.842 --> 00:01:23.886
And that's exactly what
happens in this scene.

00:01:23.886 --> 00:01:26.514
It's only very much
later in Spartan history

00:01:26.514 --> 00:01:29.558
where they say, "We don't
chase the fleeing enemy,"

00:01:29.558 --> 00:01:31.102
because if you chase the enemy,

00:01:31.102 --> 00:01:32.436
then you're caught out of formation.

00:01:32.436 --> 00:01:33.521
You become vulnerable.

00:01:33.521 --> 00:01:35.940
But at this time, the
time of the Persian War,

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.775
so the Spartans had no rules against this,

00:01:37.775 --> 00:01:39.568
and, indeed, it's a true story.

00:01:39.568 --> 00:01:41.904
You can't really dispute that,

00:01:41.904 --> 00:01:45.866
but in sense of the
tactics and the weapons,

00:01:45.866 --> 00:01:47.368
it feels like a fantasy movie.

00:01:47.368 --> 00:01:48.869
Maybe three or four out of 10.

00:01:51.122 --> 00:01:54.125
[soldiers marching]

00:01:56.669 --> 00:01:59.463
If the old man's in range,
why isn't everybody shooting?

00:01:59.463 --> 00:02:02.008
They're making all these
people on the walls

00:02:02.008 --> 00:02:03.968
hold their arrow for a really long time.

00:02:03.968 --> 00:02:05.261
And these are really heavy bows,

00:02:05.261 --> 00:02:06.846
so it's exhausting and pointless.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.979
Plate armor is intended
to keep arrows out.

00:02:14.979 --> 00:02:16.147
Bows aren't guns.

00:02:16.147 --> 00:02:18.441
They are meant as a suppressive weapon.

00:02:18.441 --> 00:02:20.401
You use it to keep their heads down.

00:02:20.401 --> 00:02:22.278
And so of course you would still use it,

00:02:22.278 --> 00:02:24.322
even against people wearing plate armor,

00:02:24.322 --> 00:02:26.282
because even the feel of arrows

00:02:26.282 --> 00:02:28.492
sort of pinging off your
armor or falling around you

00:02:28.492 --> 00:02:31.078
is going to make you much
more cautious and hesitant.

00:02:31.078 --> 00:02:33.831
[dramatic music]

00:02:37.335 --> 00:02:39.045
Obviously, attacking walls with ladders

00:02:39.045 --> 00:02:40.921
is just a really, really
risky proposition.

00:02:40.921 --> 00:02:42.214
And usually it doesn't work.

00:02:42.214 --> 00:02:44.008
If you believe they don't
have either the courage

00:02:44.008 --> 00:02:47.261
or the strength to resist
you, then you might try this.

00:02:47.261 --> 00:02:48.888
You just put a lot of
ladders against the walls,

00:02:48.888 --> 00:02:51.223
swarm up, take the place.

00:02:51.223 --> 00:02:52.600
If you expect it to be defended,

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.561
it puts you in such a
position of vulnerability,

00:02:55.561 --> 00:02:57.438
approaching the walls one at a time,

00:02:57.438 --> 00:02:59.398
and that's just not going to go well.

00:02:59.398 --> 00:03:01.108
But obviously it is a very common tactic.

00:03:01.108 --> 00:03:03.110
I mean, you can go to the
British Museum right now

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:06.238
and see depictions of people
assaulting walls with ladders.

00:03:06.238 --> 00:03:07.198
Yeah. So, I mean, the problem

00:03:07.198 --> 00:03:09.075
when you see these kind
of siege scenes in movies,

00:03:09.075 --> 00:03:10.076
that they always seem to make it

00:03:10.076 --> 00:03:11.452
really easy for the attacker.

00:03:11.452 --> 00:03:12.745
If they want to go right up to the wall,

00:03:12.745 --> 00:03:13.704
they can just do that.

00:03:13.704 --> 00:03:15.456
If they want to bring siege engines,

00:03:15.456 --> 00:03:16.540
if they want to bring catapults

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:18.292
or rams or towers up to the walls,

00:03:18.292 --> 00:03:19.377
they can just do that.

00:03:19.377 --> 00:03:21.420
It's like they're trying
to make it possible

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:22.838
for this place to fall.

00:03:22.838 --> 00:03:23.798
Whereas in reality, I mean,

00:03:23.798 --> 00:03:25.883
one of the most common
forms of fortification is,

00:03:25.883 --> 00:03:27.885
very simply, if you have
the ground like this,

00:03:27.885 --> 00:03:30.721
you dig a ditch, and you
pile up the sand behind it,

00:03:30.721 --> 00:03:33.808
then you build your wall on top of that.

00:03:33.808 --> 00:03:34.642
And now what do you have?

00:03:34.642 --> 00:03:36.685
The enemy wants to approach
you from the front.

00:03:36.685 --> 00:03:37.645
They end up in the ditch.

00:03:37.645 --> 00:03:39.897
Suddenly the wall they're
facing is much higher.

00:03:39.897 --> 00:03:42.233
If they want to bring siege
towers or battering rams,

00:03:42.233 --> 00:03:43.442
they fall into the ditch.

00:03:43.442 --> 00:03:45.444
They would have to fill
up the ditch first,

00:03:45.444 --> 00:03:46.612
before they can get to you.

00:03:46.612 --> 00:03:49.407
All that time, they're
right under your walls.

00:03:49.407 --> 00:03:50.491
In movies, they never do this

00:03:50.491 --> 00:03:52.118
because that would slow things down.

00:03:52.118 --> 00:03:53.494
They want to keep the assault engaging.

00:03:53.494 --> 00:03:55.788
That's why a lot of the
tactics that they show you

00:03:55.788 --> 00:03:57.706
are actually too simple,

00:03:57.706 --> 00:03:59.041
and they're missing a lot

00:03:59.041 --> 00:04:00.793
of the points that would actually be used.

00:04:02.753 --> 00:04:04.088
[orcs growling]

00:04:08.843 --> 00:04:11.053
Well, firstly, that
hill is much too steep,

00:04:11.053 --> 00:04:12.263
so everybody would die.

00:04:12.263 --> 00:04:14.932
But [laughs] these horses would just

00:04:14.932 --> 00:04:17.184
slide to their deaths, unfortunately.

00:04:17.184 --> 00:04:20.521
But if we take that out of the equation,

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.442
I always thought it was
validated by the fact

00:04:24.442 --> 00:04:26.861
that he uses the light of
the sun to blind the orcs,

00:04:26.861 --> 00:04:29.029
which means they lower their
pikes at the last moment.

00:04:29.029 --> 00:04:31.615
So the idea is that he
creates those openings

00:04:31.615 --> 00:04:33.200
that the horses need to see

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:34.994
in order to push their charge.

00:04:34.994 --> 00:04:37.204
So you see them sort of
wavering and breaking

00:04:37.204 --> 00:04:38.747
just before the charge hits,

00:04:38.747 --> 00:04:40.082
which is exactly the point.

00:04:40.082 --> 00:04:43.043
That makes it conceivable
that this could have worked.

00:04:43.043 --> 00:04:44.211
I mean, it is still a fantasy,

00:04:44.211 --> 00:04:45.421
and they're doing many things wrong.

00:04:45.421 --> 00:04:47.256
On the other hand,

00:04:47.256 --> 00:04:50.009
it certainly hits a number of points

00:04:50.009 --> 00:04:51.552
where you're like,
well, this is realistic,

00:04:51.552 --> 00:04:53.220
using sort of layered initiative

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:54.889
in order to overcome the defenses.

00:04:54.889 --> 00:04:57.558
Yeah. I mean, four out
of 10, five out of 10.

00:05:02.688 --> 00:05:05.399
This is just wrong.

00:05:05.399 --> 00:05:08.277
I mean, what are they doing here?

00:05:08.277 --> 00:05:10.738
Like, this is, where are
they getting the idea

00:05:10.738 --> 00:05:12.406
that the Scots in this period

00:05:12.406 --> 00:05:14.325
were wearing, like, leather and war paint?

00:05:14.325 --> 00:05:17.536
Like, these are just
ordinary, medieval infantry.

00:05:17.536 --> 00:05:20.664
They would be wearing chain
mail and helmets like this.

00:05:20.664 --> 00:05:22.833
They just deliberately
made them into savages.

00:05:22.833 --> 00:05:24.251
Why would you make them into savages?

00:05:24.251 --> 00:05:26.212
They were sophisticated people.

00:05:26.212 --> 00:05:28.589
They had disciplined pike formations.

00:05:28.589 --> 00:05:29.673
It's so bizarre.

00:05:32.092 --> 00:05:34.428
So, fire arrows are not
entirely unhistorical.

00:05:34.428 --> 00:05:36.555
They did exist, but
usually you would use them

00:05:36.555 --> 00:05:38.349
during a siege assault, for instance,

00:05:38.349 --> 00:05:40.684
like you could use them
to set things on fire,

00:05:40.684 --> 00:05:41.727
which is what they are for.

00:05:41.727 --> 00:05:44.480
So, buildings and stocks and supplies

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:47.191
would -- straw-thatched roofs.

00:05:47.191 --> 00:05:49.944
Because it would create
chaos and distractions.

00:05:49.944 --> 00:05:52.530
This idea of involving
fire in battle scenes

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:53.906
is just something that Hollywood loves.

00:05:53.906 --> 00:05:54.949
I mean, you just want to have fire

00:05:54.949 --> 00:05:56.242
because it's a nice spectacle.

00:05:57.952 --> 00:05:59.828
[soldiers yelling]

00:05:59.828 --> 00:06:01.038
Also, how would they have done this

00:06:01.038 --> 00:06:02.706
without the English noticing

00:06:02.706 --> 00:06:05.501
that there was a field of oil
or something on the ground?

00:06:05.501 --> 00:06:06.710
Like, they wouldn't have been like,

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:08.254
"Oh, that's probably there for no reason.

00:06:08.254 --> 00:06:09.922
I'm just gonna stand in front of it."

00:06:09.922 --> 00:06:12.925
[soldiers charging]

00:06:14.718 --> 00:06:16.804
They just do this, all these movies,

00:06:16.804 --> 00:06:18.347
they just sort of charge into each other,

00:06:18.347 --> 00:06:19.473
like horses' lancers.

00:06:19.473 --> 00:06:21.183
They just sort of mash into each other,

00:06:21.183 --> 00:06:23.060
like, no regard for anything.

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:25.229
And then when people ask me, like,

00:06:25.229 --> 00:06:26.522
"So, in a battle like this,

00:06:26.522 --> 00:06:28.649
how would you know which
one is on which side?

00:06:28.649 --> 00:06:31.277
Like, how would you know
who to stab, basically?"

00:06:31.277 --> 00:06:32.820
Like, yeah, you wouldn't, obviously,

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:33.821
if it went down like this,

00:06:33.821 --> 00:06:36.949
you would have no idea,
which is why it didn't.

00:06:36.949 --> 00:06:40.119
As soon as your formation
gets so messed up

00:06:40.119 --> 00:06:43.497
and so disorderly that the
enemy are in your ranks,

00:06:43.497 --> 00:06:44.582
you know you've lost.

00:06:44.582 --> 00:06:46.333
Now, you would, infantry would often

00:06:46.333 --> 00:06:47.918
charge into battle in the sense of that

00:06:47.918 --> 00:06:50.838
it would run the last distance

00:06:50.838 --> 00:06:53.799
to meet the enemy, because
everybody's afraid.

00:06:53.799 --> 00:06:55.301
Nobody wants to be there, really,

00:06:55.301 --> 00:06:57.386
because they know they're
about to be in mortal danger.

00:06:57.386 --> 00:06:58.846
So you run and you scream

00:06:58.846 --> 00:07:00.347
to get yourself through that point

00:07:00.347 --> 00:07:01.432
and to psych yourself up

00:07:01.432 --> 00:07:02.725
and to know that there's a lot of you

00:07:02.725 --> 00:07:04.602
who are all in it together.

00:07:04.602 --> 00:07:07.062
But then you'd probably, by
the best modern theories,

00:07:07.062 --> 00:07:10.274
you'd probably slow down
when you get to the enemy,

00:07:10.274 --> 00:07:12.234
and then you would start
stabbing at each other

00:07:12.234 --> 00:07:14.737
in more or less even lines,

00:07:14.737 --> 00:07:16.614
because otherwise everything would just,

00:07:16.614 --> 00:07:17.823
you would just lose control.

00:07:17.823 --> 00:07:20.200
This is a joke. I mean, like...

00:07:22.494 --> 00:07:24.246
from the equipment to the tactics,

00:07:24.246 --> 00:07:25.623
like, none of this has any bearing

00:07:25.623 --> 00:07:26.832
on what actually happened

00:07:26.832 --> 00:07:29.501
except that William Wallace was defeated.

00:07:29.501 --> 00:07:31.128
That's basically it. [laughs]

00:07:31.128 --> 00:07:32.880
Two out of 10 or one out of 10.

00:07:35.716 --> 00:07:37.676
That's one of the most common forms

00:07:37.676 --> 00:07:39.928
of ancient artillery,
is these bolt shooters.

00:07:39.928 --> 00:07:41.430
They would shoot these big rods.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:43.182
These sort of metal bolts.

00:07:43.182 --> 00:07:45.267
They're very effective.

00:07:45.267 --> 00:07:47.478
They shoot further and more
accurately than arrows.

00:07:50.522 --> 00:07:51.607
There's an account of somebody

00:07:51.607 --> 00:07:54.485
getting blown off his
feet and pinned to a tree

00:07:54.485 --> 00:07:55.486
by one of these things.

00:07:55.486 --> 00:07:57.279
They are very useful in
a siege because, again,

00:07:57.279 --> 00:07:58.572
they clear the walls.

00:07:58.572 --> 00:07:59.990
You don't want to stand there.

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:02.951
I mean, what is the point here?

00:08:02.951 --> 00:08:04.662
This forest is clearly very wet.

00:08:04.662 --> 00:08:05.871
It's not going to burn.

00:08:05.871 --> 00:08:08.999
It just looks cinematic.

00:08:12.544 --> 00:08:13.712
And they form a testudo!

00:08:13.712 --> 00:08:15.089
Of course they do.

00:08:15.089 --> 00:08:16.173
I mean, it's not ridiculous.

00:08:16.173 --> 00:08:17.675
I mean, the Romans did have a formation

00:08:17.675 --> 00:08:19.677
like that, it was called testudo,

00:08:19.677 --> 00:08:22.012
and they did use it in a few occasions

00:08:22.012 --> 00:08:23.347
when you knew you had to pass

00:08:23.347 --> 00:08:25.140
through a bunch of arrow fire

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:28.102
and you didn't want to
lose any casualties,

00:08:28.102 --> 00:08:29.561
but it's a desperate measure.

00:08:29.561 --> 00:08:31.397
So then you're stuck waiting, essentially,

00:08:31.397 --> 00:08:33.982
for the enemy to stop shooting at you.

00:08:35.109 --> 00:08:36.527
So, in a battle situation

00:08:36.527 --> 00:08:38.862
you wouldn't want to waste your time

00:08:38.862 --> 00:08:40.197
trying to get into that formation

00:08:40.197 --> 00:08:42.157
and then waste your time
trying to get out of it again

00:08:42.157 --> 00:08:43.742
when the enemy's right there.

00:08:43.742 --> 00:08:45.369
Instead, you would just try to get

00:08:45.369 --> 00:08:47.913
through that zone and
get into the thick of it.

00:08:48.789 --> 00:08:50.374
Maximus: Do not be troubled.

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:53.919
For you are in Elysium,

00:08:53.919 --> 00:08:55.337
and you're already dead!

00:08:55.337 --> 00:08:56.880
Roel: It's an endless debate
whether those speeches

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.632
actually happened, because it's hard to

00:08:58.632 --> 00:09:00.426
speak to an entire army for the people,

00:09:00.426 --> 00:09:02.219
especially without a microphone.

00:09:02.219 --> 00:09:05.180
But it is something that
is very, very common

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:06.807
in descriptions of battles.

00:09:06.807 --> 00:09:09.601
So the best we can do is
assume that they did happen

00:09:09.601 --> 00:09:11.186
and that they were a very
important part of it.

00:09:11.186 --> 00:09:14.189
[soldiers charging]

00:09:15.691 --> 00:09:20.320
So, the cavalry here bizarrely
charges through a forest.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:22.573
This is a really bad idea.

00:09:22.573 --> 00:09:25.534
Your horses are going to break their legs.

00:09:25.534 --> 00:09:27.953
Like, you'd try to find open ground.

00:09:27.953 --> 00:09:30.706
I mean, it uses a lot of

00:09:30.706 --> 00:09:32.458
actual Roman tactics and equipment,

00:09:32.458 --> 00:09:33.876
so I guess six out of 10,

00:09:33.876 --> 00:09:35.794
but it's a mishmash.

00:09:40.257 --> 00:09:41.258
The whole point is just that

00:09:41.258 --> 00:09:42.843
they're just doing "Saving Private Ryan."

00:09:42.843 --> 00:09:44.845
This is just made up because
they wanted to have a scene

00:09:44.845 --> 00:09:45.929
like "Saving Private Ryan."

00:09:45.929 --> 00:09:48.891
Resisted landings are not
common in ancient warfare,

00:09:48.891 --> 00:09:52.060
because they're very kind
of a modern warfare thing.

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:53.228
Most of the time,

00:09:53.228 --> 00:09:55.564
they would just find a
suitable beach somewhere

00:09:55.564 --> 00:09:57.357
a reasonable distance
away from their target

00:09:57.357 --> 00:09:58.233
and land there.

00:09:58.233 --> 00:09:59.943
And then they would
advance on their target.

00:10:01.528 --> 00:10:03.322
These are just stakes for show.

00:10:03.322 --> 00:10:04.531
Who are they gonna stop?

00:10:04.531 --> 00:10:07.159
Like, they're not trying to
land tanks here or anything.

00:10:07.159 --> 00:10:08.911
People can just walk through them.

00:10:08.911 --> 00:10:10.412
Like, if you want to fortify anything,

00:10:10.412 --> 00:10:13.332
in antiquity, in the Middle
Ages, in the modern day,

00:10:13.332 --> 00:10:15.083
ditch and a palisade. It's that simple.

00:10:15.083 --> 00:10:17.211
It's easy, all you need is a bit of wood

00:10:17.211 --> 00:10:19.671
and some shovels, and
they haven't done it.

00:10:19.671 --> 00:10:22.299
They just put some stakes up
here and there for no reason.

00:10:22.299 --> 00:10:25.344
Just kind of sprinkled some stakes.

00:10:28.347 --> 00:10:30.808
This kind of shield
formation is total rubbish.

00:10:30.808 --> 00:10:32.100
I mean, come on.

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:33.769
There's nothing like
that in Greek history.

00:10:33.769 --> 00:10:35.270
They're not going to be like this

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:37.231
overlapping sort of turtle thing.

00:10:40.275 --> 00:10:41.985
In the period where this poem is set,

00:10:41.985 --> 00:10:43.821
there was no mounted cavalry.

00:10:43.821 --> 00:10:45.030
And even in later periods,

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:47.658
you rarely have people
actually fighting on horseback.

00:10:47.658 --> 00:10:52.037
It's hard to work out how
to use cavalry tactically.

00:10:52.037 --> 00:10:53.038
It's difficult to use it

00:10:53.038 --> 00:10:54.915
if you don't have very many of them,

00:10:54.915 --> 00:10:56.834
and horses are just very precious.

00:10:56.834 --> 00:10:59.586
It costs an enormous amount
of money to maintain a horse,

00:10:59.586 --> 00:11:01.129
so you don't want to waste your horse

00:11:01.129 --> 00:11:03.507
on something like this guy and his hammer.

00:11:07.845 --> 00:11:09.096
Charging all together.

00:11:09.096 --> 00:11:10.973
They have no sense of order.

00:11:10.973 --> 00:11:13.976
And, in fact, if you look
closely in one of these scenes,

00:11:13.976 --> 00:11:15.894
just to the left of Paris,

00:11:15.894 --> 00:11:18.105
after the next shot of
Paris running away, I think.

00:11:21.733 --> 00:11:24.778
[laughing] The extra
falls down on his face.

00:11:24.778 --> 00:11:26.572
I mean, I love that, because
it just shows exactly

00:11:26.572 --> 00:11:28.574
why you wouldn't want
to be doing this, right?

00:11:28.574 --> 00:11:31.660
Because if everybody's
running all together,

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:33.453
you're just going to trip over yourself.

00:11:33.453 --> 00:11:35.289
Like, everybody gets in each other's way.

00:11:35.289 --> 00:11:36.748
Like, they're doing a beach landing,

00:11:36.748 --> 00:11:39.251
which is obviously inspired
by an earlier movie.

00:11:39.251 --> 00:11:40.669
It has nothing to do with history

00:11:40.669 --> 00:11:42.838
except that they have swords and arrows.

00:11:42.838 --> 00:11:45.090
Yeah, I mean, what do
you want? Two out of 10?

00:11:48.218 --> 00:11:49.720
So, again, where is your ditch?

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:51.263
Where is your ditch?!

00:11:51.263 --> 00:11:52.556
You've got to have a ditch!

00:11:52.556 --> 00:11:54.308
Otherwise they're going
to run those siege towers

00:11:54.308 --> 00:11:56.310
right up to your wall. You know this!

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:58.520
And obviously Jerusalem at
the time did have a ditch,

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:01.273
and it had a pre-wall and another ditch,

00:12:01.273 --> 00:12:02.399
so that you can't just

00:12:02.399 --> 00:12:04.943
put your siege engines
right up to the wall.

00:12:04.943 --> 00:12:06.361
[hammer clanging]

00:12:06.361 --> 00:12:08.322
Of course they start throwing fireballs.

00:12:09.406 --> 00:12:11.199
[sighs]

00:12:11.199 --> 00:12:13.243
Stop it with the fireballs!

00:12:13.243 --> 00:12:16.246
[soldiers shouting]

00:12:18.624 --> 00:12:20.167
Admittedly, it is realistic here

00:12:20.167 --> 00:12:22.252
that they are very concerned about

00:12:22.252 --> 00:12:25.005
trying to keep their siege
weapon from catching fire.

00:12:25.005 --> 00:12:26.840
The defenders would try
to set them on fire,

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:29.009
and so it's very important
to protect them from that,

00:12:29.009 --> 00:12:31.678
usually by covering them with wet hides.

00:12:31.678 --> 00:12:34.306
[soldiers shouting]

00:12:34.306 --> 00:12:35.515
[oil burbling]

00:12:35.515 --> 00:12:37.309
So, boiling oil is a big trope, right?

00:12:37.309 --> 00:12:38.477
You see it in a lot of movies.

00:12:38.477 --> 00:12:41.605
It's not a thing. Didn't happen. Sorry.

00:12:41.605 --> 00:12:43.357
So, there's no evidence
of it ever happening.

00:12:43.357 --> 00:12:44.858
There's no description of people

00:12:44.858 --> 00:12:46.610
being smothered in boiling oil.

00:12:46.610 --> 00:12:49.154
Certainly not oil that's
already been set on fire.

00:12:49.154 --> 00:12:51.239
I mean, you can just throw boiling water

00:12:51.239 --> 00:12:53.367
and it does the same thing,
which is hurt people,

00:12:53.367 --> 00:12:54.368
or you can just throw rocks.

00:12:54.368 --> 00:12:55.452
But they cost you nothing,

00:12:55.452 --> 00:12:57.120
they take no preparation,

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:58.997
and you just throw them at people.

00:12:58.997 --> 00:13:00.082
They get hurt.

00:13:00.082 --> 00:13:01.041
It's great.

00:13:01.041 --> 00:13:03.669
So boiling oil is just
needlessly elaborate,

00:13:03.669 --> 00:13:04.628
and you're burning fuel.

00:13:04.628 --> 00:13:06.922
You don't want to be burning
things for no good reason.

00:13:06.922 --> 00:13:08.465
In the account that we have of this siege,

00:13:08.465 --> 00:13:11.301
it says there was constant
fighting outside the walls,

00:13:11.301 --> 00:13:13.095
and there were also assaults on the walls.

00:13:13.095 --> 00:13:14.846
This one's better than
the other one, actually.

00:13:14.846 --> 00:13:15.931
Better than Helm's Deep.

00:13:20.978 --> 00:13:23.021
Again, they should have
been digging ditches.

00:13:23.021 --> 00:13:25.107
Literally all they do is just stand around

00:13:25.107 --> 00:13:27.442
carrying, like, spears
from one point to another.

00:13:27.442 --> 00:13:29.820
You should be digging
ditches. Many ditches.

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:31.947
Once your first ditch is
ready, build another one.

00:13:36.743 --> 00:13:37.953
The female characters,

00:13:37.953 --> 00:13:39.496
other than the ones who
have been trained to fight,

00:13:39.496 --> 00:13:40.622
are basically just sort of hiding

00:13:40.622 --> 00:13:42.666
in the dungeon and not doing anything.

00:13:42.666 --> 00:13:43.667
In actual sieges,

00:13:43.667 --> 00:13:45.168
especially when it was this desperate,

00:13:45.168 --> 00:13:47.170
I mean, they would just
be expected to contribute.

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:49.798
Women, children, old men,
everybody does something,

00:13:49.798 --> 00:13:51.466
because there's always
things that people can do

00:13:51.466 --> 00:13:52.551
even when they don't fight.

00:13:52.551 --> 00:13:54.469
You know, go and carry arrows and stones

00:13:54.469 --> 00:13:55.721
up to the people on the walls,

00:13:55.721 --> 00:13:59.850
go put out fires, help
with water and food,

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:01.435
help with clothing and the wounded.

00:14:01.435 --> 00:14:03.270
There is no way you would
just stick them away

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:04.521
in a corner somewhere.

00:14:08.859 --> 00:14:10.444
These catapults are
obviously in the wrong place.

00:14:10.444 --> 00:14:11.403
They should be on top of the wall.

00:14:11.403 --> 00:14:13.238
Like, why would you put
them outside of the walls?

00:14:13.238 --> 00:14:14.322
They're going to be overrun.

00:14:14.322 --> 00:14:15.949
You can't move them out
of the way quickly enough,

00:14:15.949 --> 00:14:17.409
so put them on top of the wall.

00:14:17.409 --> 00:14:18.994
That's where you put your artillery.

00:14:22.289 --> 00:14:24.207
So, you can put your cavalry in front,

00:14:24.207 --> 00:14:26.043
and it totally works,

00:14:26.043 --> 00:14:29.212
if you don't expect your
enemy to stand their ground.

00:14:29.212 --> 00:14:31.840
So, putting your cavalry
in front is a time-tested,

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:34.134
very common strategy, totally works,

00:14:34.134 --> 00:14:36.303
unless you're fighting ice zombies,

00:14:36.303 --> 00:14:38.430
who are not going to break,
and they're not scared of you.

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:40.015
You're just wasting your cavalry.

00:14:40.015 --> 00:14:42.809
So it's the right tactic
for the wrong moment.

00:14:42.809 --> 00:14:44.478
There are probably several battles,

00:14:44.478 --> 00:14:45.687
other battles in "Game of Thrones"

00:14:45.687 --> 00:14:46.855
where this could have worked really well.

00:14:46.855 --> 00:14:48.148
Should you find yourself

00:14:48.148 --> 00:14:50.067
fighting an army of ice zombies,

00:14:50.067 --> 00:14:51.568
so, put your artillery on the wall,

00:14:51.568 --> 00:14:53.612
dig many ditches,

00:14:53.612 --> 00:14:55.989
just lots and lots of ditches.

00:14:55.989 --> 00:14:58.283
You can put, of course,
your infantry in front,

00:14:58.283 --> 00:14:59.659
if you want to confront them there,

00:14:59.659 --> 00:15:01.703
because that means that you will have

00:15:01.703 --> 00:15:03.497
several stages to fall back on.

00:15:03.497 --> 00:15:06.917
After that, you won't sort of
blow everything all in one go.

00:15:06.917 --> 00:15:10.670
But put your cavalry
away from the main line

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:12.839
so that once the enemy is engaged,

00:15:12.839 --> 00:15:15.342
once your ice zombies are
stuck against your line,

00:15:15.342 --> 00:15:17.385
you can then swoop in from the side,

00:15:17.385 --> 00:15:20.514
where they are not expecting
you, and overrun them.

00:15:20.514 --> 00:15:22.641
I mean, it's not going
to really necessarily

00:15:22.641 --> 00:15:24.851
win your fight against
an army of ice zombies,

00:15:24.851 --> 00:15:26.728
but at least it means
that you are fighting them

00:15:26.728 --> 00:15:28.188
in a way that they don't expect.

00:15:28.188 --> 00:15:29.314
At least you will have done something

00:15:29.314 --> 00:15:30.941
with your cavalry that made sense.

00:15:30.941 --> 00:15:33.485
But otherwise, I mean,
obviously it's all a bit silly.

00:15:33.485 --> 00:15:36.738
So, what should we say, five out of 10?

00:15:36.738 --> 00:15:39.491
[dramatic music]

00:15:45.205 --> 00:15:47.916
I mean, this movie had an Oxford professor

00:15:47.916 --> 00:15:50.335
advising on the movie.

00:15:50.335 --> 00:15:52.879
You can see all the little
details that they got

00:15:52.879 --> 00:15:53.797
in terms of their dress,

00:15:53.797 --> 00:15:55.590
in terms of their tactics and maneuvers.

00:15:55.590 --> 00:15:56.883
Like, all of this is

00:15:56.883 --> 00:15:58.510
as good as we can get it, almost.

00:15:58.510 --> 00:16:01.221
It's really, really precise,

00:16:01.221 --> 00:16:03.974
except for the fact that,
for the Persian Army,

00:16:03.974 --> 00:16:05.392
they still went with this kind of

00:16:05.392 --> 00:16:09.813
slightly racist depiction of, you know,

00:16:09.813 --> 00:16:12.524
flimsy warriors wearing
eyeliner and things like that.

00:16:12.524 --> 00:16:15.068
It doesn't really reflect
reality at this point.

00:16:15.068 --> 00:16:16.486
They would have been much more organized

00:16:16.486 --> 00:16:18.363
and much more heavily equipped,

00:16:18.363 --> 00:16:20.782
but this is just the way
that people like to depict

00:16:20.782 --> 00:16:23.660
Persia in general and the East.

00:16:23.660 --> 00:16:26.329
It's just...it leaves a
bad taste in your mouth.

00:16:26.329 --> 00:16:28.915
[soldiers charging]

00:16:28.915 --> 00:16:30.834
In fact, throughout this period,

00:16:30.834 --> 00:16:34.379
the Greeks were the ones
who fought like that,

00:16:34.379 --> 00:16:37.048
whereas the Persians were
known to be much more organized

00:16:37.048 --> 00:16:39.050
and to advance silently into battle,

00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:40.051
which was something that the Greeks

00:16:40.051 --> 00:16:41.303
were really impressed by.

00:16:43.889 --> 00:16:46.808
Camels, always useful,
because they scare horses.

00:16:46.808 --> 00:16:48.810
It's kind of a theme
throughout Persian history.

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:51.062
They would use camels to
scare off enemy cavalry.

00:16:54.649 --> 00:16:56.818
With this movie, like, all
the equipment is just right.

00:16:56.818 --> 00:16:59.446
Like, these guys, you know
these are the Companions.

00:16:59.446 --> 00:17:01.823
You know, later on, there are
going to be some Thracians.

00:17:01.823 --> 00:17:04.326
Like, you can pinpoint
very exactly who they are.

00:17:05.202 --> 00:17:08.246
The whole pike phalanx is
exactly depicted accurately,

00:17:08.246 --> 00:17:10.207
with all of the weaponry,

00:17:10.207 --> 00:17:11.499
the spacing between the men,

00:17:11.499 --> 00:17:13.418
and the kind of blocks
in which they maneuver

00:17:13.418 --> 00:17:14.252
and things like that.

00:17:19.674 --> 00:17:20.675
Now, this is obviously a bit silly.

00:17:20.675 --> 00:17:22.427
People wouldn't deliberately
impale themselves

00:17:22.427 --> 00:17:24.262
on a giant stick. It's much more likely

00:17:24.262 --> 00:17:25.263
that they would just sort of

00:17:25.263 --> 00:17:28.308
stop in front of that
and start falling back.

00:17:28.308 --> 00:17:30.268
And you don't actually
expect a big pike formation

00:17:30.268 --> 00:17:31.895
to actually kill all those people,

00:17:31.895 --> 00:17:35.607
because people don't, you
know, they try not to die.

00:17:35.607 --> 00:17:37.317
They're going to run away from it.

00:17:38.485 --> 00:17:40.528
I mean, this is exactly
as it says in the sources.

00:17:40.528 --> 00:17:41.821
This is our best reconstruction

00:17:41.821 --> 00:17:42.906
of how this battle happened.

00:17:42.906 --> 00:17:44.783
This is, in terms of ancient warfare,

00:17:44.783 --> 00:17:46.034
this is the most accurate depiction

00:17:46.034 --> 00:17:47.035
that you'll find anywhere,

00:17:47.035 --> 00:17:48.536
and I would give this nine out of 10.

00:17:51.248 --> 00:17:53.333
Kill them all!

00:17:53.333 --> 00:17:56.127
[soldiers charging]

00:17:56.127 --> 00:17:57.587
Roel: Stop! No!

00:17:57.587 --> 00:17:58.797
Where are your missiles?

00:17:58.797 --> 00:18:00.131
Now, this is where -- [sighs]

00:18:00.131 --> 00:18:02.300
These battles, they would be about

00:18:02.300 --> 00:18:03.843
trying to disrupt the formation,

00:18:03.843 --> 00:18:06.888
not trying to slam into
it and hope it goes away.

00:18:06.888 --> 00:18:08.056
I mean, you'd be throwing,

00:18:08.056 --> 00:18:09.224
you'd be shooting arrows at it

00:18:09.224 --> 00:18:10.433
and throwing javelins at it

00:18:10.433 --> 00:18:12.811
to try and see if you can
create some gaps in it.

00:18:12.811 --> 00:18:14.896
And then eventually,
once you saw a weak spot,

00:18:14.896 --> 00:18:16.189
you might sort of rush forward.

00:18:16.189 --> 00:18:18.650
And so you could use your
swords and your spears

00:18:18.650 --> 00:18:20.694
to try and create a hole
in the shield walls.

00:18:20.694 --> 00:18:22.696
It doesn't do any, it
doesn't achieve anything.

00:18:24.114 --> 00:18:26.116
Yeah, no, Vikings would
definitely use shield walls.

00:18:26.116 --> 00:18:28.034
I mean, that aspect of it is realistic,

00:18:28.034 --> 00:18:30.370
except the fact that it's
a three-layered shield wall

00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:32.539
seems a bit excessive.

00:18:32.539 --> 00:18:34.582
That's a Roman formation.

00:18:34.582 --> 00:18:37.627
But you do have, obviously
Viking shield walls

00:18:37.627 --> 00:18:40.255
are the way they would fight
pitched battles when they did.

00:18:40.255 --> 00:18:41.881
So they would try to avoid pitched battles

00:18:41.881 --> 00:18:42.966
and mostly would fight

00:18:42.966 --> 00:18:45.635
in surprise attacks, ambushes, and sieges.

00:18:45.635 --> 00:18:47.721
So this is a rare occasion where,

00:18:47.721 --> 00:18:48.722
according to this scene,

00:18:48.722 --> 00:18:50.724
apparently they actually
had numerical superiority,

00:18:50.724 --> 00:18:51.683
so then they could risk it.

00:18:51.683 --> 00:18:52.851
So, this one, I would probably give, like,

00:18:52.851 --> 00:18:54.185
six or seven out of 10.

00:18:56.771 --> 00:19:00.025
And so, this movie is
entirely based on the romance.

00:19:00.025 --> 00:19:01.484
So, not on the historical record.

00:19:01.484 --> 00:19:03.153
It's essentially just a fantasy story.

00:19:05.322 --> 00:19:06.948
This formation, [laughs]

00:19:06.948 --> 00:19:07.907
in the historical record,

00:19:07.907 --> 00:19:11.202
there is something called
the eight-gates formation,

00:19:11.202 --> 00:19:12.912
which isn't described.

00:19:12.912 --> 00:19:16.499
It means a really elaborate
infantry formation

00:19:16.499 --> 00:19:19.544
that has these sort of
changing, shifting shield walls,

00:19:19.544 --> 00:19:22.213
which is intended to trap enemy cavalry.

00:19:22.213 --> 00:19:23.840
And so that's what you're
seeing happening here,

00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:25.884
is that they are using
shifting shield walls

00:19:25.884 --> 00:19:26.801
to trap enemy cavalry.

00:19:26.801 --> 00:19:28.511
There are a couple of
descriptions of it happening

00:19:28.511 --> 00:19:31.431
in a sort of competitive maneuvering,

00:19:31.431 --> 00:19:33.016
where two armies are
sort of trying to show

00:19:33.016 --> 00:19:35.018
that they have more
control and more discipline

00:19:35.018 --> 00:19:37.687
by competitively moving troops around.

00:19:38.813 --> 00:19:40.231
But actually there is no evidence

00:19:40.231 --> 00:19:42.984
that it was ever used in
a battle to defeat anyone.

00:19:42.984 --> 00:19:45.320
It's much too complex.

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:47.447
The amount of control that you would need

00:19:47.447 --> 00:19:49.157
over every individual detachment,

00:19:49.157 --> 00:19:51.993
the amount of coordination
of each single man,

00:19:51.993 --> 00:19:54.329
everybody has to know
exactly where he has to stand

00:19:54.329 --> 00:19:55.830
for this to work.

00:19:55.830 --> 00:19:57.332
And none of them can, you know,

00:19:57.332 --> 00:19:59.376
even a stray arrow killing one of them

00:19:59.376 --> 00:20:00.835
would disrupt this entire formation

00:20:00.835 --> 00:20:02.754
because it would create a gap.

00:20:02.754 --> 00:20:04.798
So it's just not feasible.

00:20:04.798 --> 00:20:06.549
That seems sort of wildly optimistic

00:20:06.549 --> 00:20:09.052
as to the abilities of any infantry force.

00:20:10.303 --> 00:20:12.722
[drum beating]

00:20:15.517 --> 00:20:17.394
In real life, if you could
get that close to cavalry,

00:20:17.394 --> 00:20:18.269
why not just stab them?

00:20:18.269 --> 00:20:20.480
It's another one of those
things that is based on a story,

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:21.940
so how can you judge it?

00:20:21.940 --> 00:20:24.067
Like, it's cool, it's evocative,

00:20:24.067 --> 00:20:25.568
but it's not historical.

00:20:25.568 --> 00:20:26.778
It's got the equipment right,

00:20:26.778 --> 00:20:27.862
so maybe four.

